Spilling capture state to disk
==============================

This document describes why and how TShark can keep the dissection state of a
capture file on disk instead of in RAM (the --spill-dir option), what it does
not cover, and what the measured cost is. For the mechanics of the allocator
itself see section 4.1.1 of README.wmem and the doxygen comments in
wsutil/wmem/wmem_backing.h.

1. The problem
--------------

Analysis state grows with the capture, and when it no longer fits in RAM the
process dies. There is no partial result and no way to trade speed for
capacity: g_malloc() fails, GLib aborts, and nothing is produced.

Measured on a 4.1 GB / 9.53 M packet capture of real traffic, with every
malloc-family allocation accounted:

    peak heap, single pass                       9089 MB
    peak heap, two-pass (-2)                    10184 MB

Attributing that by workload (flow-complete subsets of the same traffic, and
synthetic captures that vary one dimension at a time):

    per TCP conversation                     ~4.0   KB
    per packet, TCP sequence analysis        ~138   B
    per packet, frame_data (two-pass only)    112   B
    per packet, DNS-only traffic             ~600   B
    per packet, HTTP-only traffic           ~1400   B
    fixed floor (dissector registration)      ~91   MB

So capacity was roughly RAM/1070 B per packet for a mixed workload. The
existing mitigation, -M, periodically destroys the whole session: it bounds
memory by discarding conversations, reassembly and all other per-capture
state, which changes the results, and it is incompatible with -2.

2. Goal
-------

Make the RAM requirement independent of the capture size, accepting that the
run gets slower, and without changing any output. Specifically:

  - identical results with and without the option, including taps and -2;
  - no state discarded, unlike -M;
  - no per-dissector changes, so the 3000+ dissectors are unaffected.

Out of scope: live capture (state is bounded by the capture duration, not
the file) and parallel dissection (an orthogonal track). The Qt GUI takes
the same options; what it still pays for separately is in section 8.

3. Architecture
---------------

A backing store (wmem_backing_t) is one or more large shared mappings of a
sparse temporary file. Allocators hand out regions of them instead of
regions of the heap. Because a mapping is made once and never moved,
addresses are stable for the life of the store, so nothing above the
allocator - not the dissectors, not the data structures, not even the
allocator's own chunk logic - has to know where the memory lives. Pages are
resident only while in use; under memory pressure the kernel writes them to
the backing file rather than failing the allocation.

The store records its regions - each either handed out or free - as file
offsets in a shared anonymous mapping, with a PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED
mutex around them. A region being freed is merged with its neighbours,
and a request is served from the smallest free region that holds it,
which matters for processes that outlive one capture file because jumbo
allocations are page-rounded to sizes that need not ever recur. Keeping
the bookkeeping where processes share it rather than on the private heap
is what lets a process that forks after the store is created allocate
from the same store; offsets rather than addresses mean no process has
to map a piece of the file where any other one did. When nothing mapped
can hold a request, the store maps another range rather than failing, so
--spill-reserve is a starting size and not a ceiling.

The block allocator took all of its OS memory from exactly three places
(8 MB blocks, jumbo allocations, and their frees), so pointing it at a store
is a policy change, not a new allocator: wmem_allocator_new_backed() passes a
store down to wmem_block_allocator_init_backed(), and everything else about
the allocator is unchanged. With a NULL store the behaviour is exactly as
before.

TShark and Wireshark create one store from --spill-dir before epan_init()
and attach it to the allocators whose contents live as long as the capture
file:

    file scope            conversations, TCP analysis, per-packet proto data,
                          streaming reassembly, reassembly tables and keys,
                          reassembled buffers
    addr_resolv_scope     name resolution caches
    frame_data_sequence   the per-frame index (two-pass analysis, and every
                          capture opened in the GUI)
    fragment_data_scope   copies of individual fragment payloads

Epan scope is never backed: it holds the dissector registration tables, which
are always hot and are a fixed cost.

4. Lifetime segregation
-----------------------

The last two arenas exist because a block allocator never returns a freed
chunk to the OS - only whole blocks that have become entirely free, and only
on garbage collection. Mixing allocations with different lifetimes in one
arena therefore pins it at the sum of the high-water marks of all of them.

Reassembly is the case where this bites. The fragment payload copies are
dropped as soon as their reassembly completes, while the reassembled output
and the bookkeeping live until the capture is closed. Putting both in file
scope raised total footprint on an HTTP capture from 4804 MB to 8900 MB.
Giving the fragment copies their own arena, collected every 4096 releases,
brought it back to 4754 MB: blocks go empty, garbage collection returns them
to the store, and the store releases the pages (and, where the platform
supports hole punching, the disk blocks).

This is the general rule for anything added later: an arena should hold
objects with one lifetime.

5. What is still on the heap
----------------------------

    dissector registration tables (epan scope)        ~91 MB, fixed
    tap accumulators                                  grows with the number
                                                      of conversations
    GHashTable internals of the reassembly tables     grows with fragments
    wiretap read buffers                              bounded per packet
    Qt packet list records (GUI only)                 ~180 B per packet

Measured residual on the 4.1 GB capture with -z conv,tcp: 141 MB, against
8954 MB of state in the store.

6. Correctness
--------------

The option changes where memory comes from, never what is computed, so the
verification is equality of output rather than a new set of expectations:

  - test/captures, 339 real captures: -V -x and -2 -V -x byte-identical with
    and without --spill-dir;
  - 9.53 M packet capture: -z conv,tcp output identical, 149018 rows;
  - -T fields, -T json, -2 -T json, -z endpoints,ip identical;
  - the project test suite (2089 tests) and reassemble_test unchanged;
  - repeated session teardown and rebuild, which is what the GUI does on
    every preference change: -M 10000 over a 300 k packet capture (30
    resets) gives identical -z conv,tcp output with and without the store;
  - Wireshark with the same capture, --spill-after 0: 197 MB of dissection
    state moves out of the heap into the backing file (vmmap: 339 MB dirty
    malloc without the option, 128 MB malloc plus 197 MB resident file
    pages with it);
  - wmem_test runs its full allocator torture suite, including jumbo
    allocations and the invariant checker, against a file-backed allocator
    (/wmem/allocator/blk_backed) and asserts the store is fully reclaimed;
    /wmem/backing/reuse and /wmem/backing/grow cover reusing space at a
    size it was not freed at, and outgrowing the mapped range.

The one behavioural subtlety is teardown order: a tvb wrapping reassembly
data can be freed after file scope is gone, so the free callback checks
wmem_in_scope() first.

7. Cost
-------

Nothing is written while state is under the --spill-after watermark, so a
capture that fits in RAM is unaffected. Past it, the cost is page faults and
writeback on the part that went to disk, because eviction is page-granular
while the access pattern is flow-granular.

The capture used below is five days of the 2015-03 traffic merged into one
file (mergecap of 2015-03-05 through 2015-03-09): 1589 MB, 2033 k packets,
2703 MB of dissection state under -z conv,tcp. At different watermarks, with
every malloc-family allocation accounted by the interposer in test/benchmark:

    watermark      in RAM    spilled     wall   peak heap
    no spilling        -          -      9.8 s    2773 MB
    8192 MB       2703 MB       0 MB     9.0 s    2773 MB
    1024 MB       1016 MB    1687 MB    18.0 s    1083 MB
       0 MB            0    2703 MB     40.2 s      67 MB

So the penalty tracks how much is on disk, and the watermark is the knob that
trades speed for capacity rather than a switch that makes everything slow.
Every run produced -z conv,tcp output identical (cmp) to the run with no
spilling.

Flow-sharded parallelism helps more than it looks like it should, because it
shrinks the working set each process touches. The same capture printed as
packet summaries, one process against --jobs 4 (which taps cannot use yet, so
this is the printing path rather than -z conv,tcp):

    1 process, no spilling                      10.9 s
    4 workers, no spilling                       5.7 s
    4 workers, watermark 0 (everything spills)   5.9 s

Each worker held 605 to 1194 MB of state, and the sharded output was
identical with and without spilling. Spilling all of it costs 4% there
against 4.1x for one process spilling the same total state: one process
randomly touching a 2.7 GB arena misses cache constantly, four touching
sub-gigabyte arenas do not.

Other shapes, at watermark 0 (worst case), against the same run without
--spill-dir:

    capture                            in RAM     spilled   spilled   peak
                                                             state    heap
    synthetic 2 M packets, 50 k flows   5.1 s      6.4 s      688 MB   63 MB
    synthetic 1 M packets, 500 k flows  3.7 s     45.9 s     2240 MB  232 MB

(macOS 27.0.1 on APFS, Apple M4, 10 cores, 16 GB.)

The second row is the adversarial case: 500 k flows touched round-robin, so
no flow is ever in cache, and the run is almost entirely system time
faulting pages back. MADV_RANDOM is what keeps it to that, rather than
having readahead multiply every fault.

8. Limits and next steps
------------------------

Capacity. With the heap term now flat, the limits are disk, tap accumulators
for runs that use -z on very large captures, and 32-bit frame numbers.
--spill-reserve is not one of them: it is how much address space is mapped
at a time (64 GB by default), and the store maps another range of that size
when the mapped ones are full, so it only has to be raised to reduce the
number of mappings.

Speed. Page-granular eviction against flow-granular access is the remaining
problem. The fix is to make file scope resolve to a per-conversation
sub-arena, so a cold flow's state is page-contiguous and faults back as one
unit; that also makes explicit LRU eviction of idle flows possible, which
would subsume -M without discarding anything that is still reachable.

Platforms. The store needs mmap() of a sparse file, a mapping shared
across fork(), and a process-shared mutex, so it is POSIX-only;
wmem_backing_file_new() fails on Windows and --spill-dir reports that
and exits rather than silently running without it. Porting it means a
second implementation on CreateFileMapping()/MapViewOfFile() with
FSCTL_SET_ZERO_DATA for the holes, and, since there is no fork(), a
named mapping plus a named mutex inherited by re-executed workers
before --jobs could share one store there. Hole punching is already
per-platform (F_PUNCHHOLE on macOS, FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE on Linux,
and a filesystem that cannot do it just keeps the blocks).

Flow sharding attacks the same locality problem from the other side, by
making each process's arena smaller, and --jobs now does it: see
README.flow-sharding. The store is created before the workers are
forked and all of them allocate from it, so --spill-after stays a
watermark for the run and the worker holding the largest conversation
can use as much of it as that conversation needs.

GUI. Wireshark takes the same three options and creates the store where
TShark does, before epan_init() in ui/qt/main.cpp, and needs nothing else.
Random access to an arbitrary packet does not need dissection state as of
that packet: cf_select_packet() seeks to the record and calls
epan_dissect_run() against the accumulated state with fd->visited already
set (file.c), exactly as TShark's second pass does. What makes the result
right for that packet is that the first pass memoized it - TCP analysis in
tcpd->acked_table, reassembly in reassembled_table, dissector scratch in
fd->pfd - all keyed by frame number, and that conversation lookup is
scoped by frame number. Spilling discards nothing, so every one of those
lookups still finds what it found before.

Two things did have to change for a process that outlives one capture
file, both in the store rather than in the GUI: free space is merged and
split rather than reused only at the size it was freed at, because the GUI
rebuilds file scope on every preference change and jumbo allocations are
never the same size twice; and exhausting the mapped range maps more
instead of calling ws_error(), because aborting a GUI with an open capture
is not an option.

What spilling does not fix there is the Qt side: one PacketListRecord per
packet, about 180 bytes with the row vectors, which is the dominant term
above roughly 10 M packets. Interactive latency is the other cost -
scrolling past the column cache re-dissects a row, and changing the
display filter re-dissects the whole file, so both can now fault pages
back from disk.

Two gaps remain on the GUI side. There is no equivalent of TShark's
report of peak state at exit, so there is nothing to tune --spill-after
against; the natural home for it is the capture file properties dialog.
And the interactive paths above have not been measured, because driving
the widgets needs a display: the figures here come from running the GUI
with the offscreen platform plugin and reading vmmap.

9. Platform notes
-----------------

Linux releases pages with MADV_DONTNEED and punches the disk blocks out with
fallocate(FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE); macOS uses fcntl(F_PUNCHHOLE). Note that
FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE is declared in <linux/falloc.h>, not <fcntl.h>, and
fallocate() itself needs _GNU_SOURCE - get either wrong and the hole punching
silently compiles out while everything still works, just without returning
disk blocks. Windows has no equivalent of the mapping scheme used here, so
wmem_backing_file_new() fails there and --spill-dir reports the error.

Both paths are verified: on ext4 the store's file is sparse - 64 GB of
address space against the few hundred megabytes actually in use - and holding
512 MB and then freeing it returns exactly 512 MB of free space, so the
blocks really do come back. The shared bookkeeping works the same on both:
four forked workers allocating from one store give byte-identical output on
Linux and macOS alike.

Resident set size is a misleading measure on both platforms: resident pages
of the backing file count towards it even though they are evictable without
swap. On macOS use "peak memory footprint" or the malloc interposer in
test/benchmark, which fails allocations past a budget and so makes running
out of memory deterministic; on Linux a cgroup limit does the same thing
without a shim.

That interposer is also the clearest demonstration of what the option buys.
On the merged 2015-03-05..09 capture of section 7 (2033 k packets, 2703 MB
of state) with a 1024 MB malloc budget, -q -z conv,tcp:

    unmodified                        dies after 2.8 s, nothing produced
                                      ("GLib ERROR failed to allocate
                                      8388608 bytes", abort)
    --spill-dir /tmp/spill            dies the same way after 2.8 s
    --spill-dir --spill-after 512     completes in 14.4 s, peak heap 575 MB,
                                      507 MB in RAM and 2196 MB spilled

The last run's output is byte-identical (cmp) to an unconstrained run. The
middle row is the trap: --spill-after defaults to 2048 MB, which is above
the 1024 MB budget, so the store spends its heap allowance first and dies
exactly like the unmodified run. Any such demonstration has to set the
watermark below the limit it is imposing.

10. Reproducing the measurements
--------------------------------

test/benchmark contains the capture generators, the malloc-budget interposer
and a Dockerfile for the Linux side, including the demonstrations that an
unmodified run dies where a spilled run completes with identical output.
See test/benchmark/README.md, whose first section is a two-second version of
that demonstration - for TShark and for the GUI, under a malloc budget on
macOS and a cgroup limit on Linux - on a synthetic capture rather than the
multi-gigabyte ones the numbers above come from.
